{"text": "Lorilterd\n\nMEMORANDUM\n\nFebruary 26, 1997\n\nTo: See List\nFrom: \u00a7. Smith\nRe: OVERVIEW OF 120MM SEGMENT\n\n=_\u2014\n\n\u2018The purpose of this report is to provide an overview of the 120MM segment to serve as a\nfoundation for the development of additional entries, made possible by our purchase of new\nmanufacturing equipment.\n\nData covered include the following volume and share trends:\n\nHistorical 120MM Trends (from 1974 introduction thru 1996)\n\n~ By Brand\n\u2014 By Filtration Category\n\u2014 By Soft Pack Vs. Box\n\u2014 ByNon\u2014Menthol Vs. Menthol\n\nCurrent 120MM Brand Profiles and Recent Trends (1994 thru 1996)\n\n~ Lotar Brands (67% of segment)\n\u2014 Lofi Brands (22% of segment)\n\u2014 Ultra Lotar Brands (11% of segment)\n\nCurrent 120MM Development by Region (per 1996 EXCEL data)\n\nHISTORICAL TRENDS\nKey Findings\n\nThe first 120MM cigarette was introduced in 1975. Over the next 21 years, this \"longest length\"\n\u2018segment expanded to include 9 brands having a total of 24 packings. However, share of all\n120\u2019s has never exceeded 2.5% with new entries generally gaining volume at the expense of\nolder brands.\n\nlotar and box packings which are\n\n\u2018The 120MM segment has become heavily skewed towai\nmost preferred by female smokers. racidifion, 176 17d\n\nalways been much higher the Industry overall.\n\nLOZ89L1Lg",
